WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:09.943
The journey to London took five hours. When I arrived at Mr. Jaggers' office, he told me

2
00:00:09.944 --> 00:00:14.602
that I was not to go directly to the home of Matthew Pocket, my tutor. Instead I was

3
00:00:14.603 --> 00:00:22.674
to spend a few days in London with Pocket's son, Herbert. Mr. Jaggers' clerk walked me to

4
00:00:22.675 --> 00:00:27.761
young Pocket's rooms in Barnard's Inn, a collection of shabby buildings squeezed together along the street. We

5
00:00:27.762 --> 00:00:33.182
climbed the stairs to Pocket's door only to find a sign: "Return Shortly." The clerk left me,

6
00:00:33.183 --> 00:00:39.174
and I waited for half an hour before I heard Pocket's footsteps on the stairs. He had a

7
00:00:39.175 --> 00:00:41.579
paper bag under each arm and a box of strawberries in one hand, and he was out

8
00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.182
of breath. "Mr. Pip?" said he. "Mr. Pocket?" said I. It was not until we entered his

9
00:00:44.183 --> 00:00:50.507
rooms and got a good look at each other that we realized we'd met before. "You're the boy

10
00:00:50.508 --> 00:00:56.579
who was prowling about Miss Havisham's house!" said Herbert Pocket, putting down his bags of food. "And

11
00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.325
you are the pale young gentleman who encouraged me to fight!" "Well," said Herbert as he reached

12
00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:08.833
for my hand, "that's all over now. I hope you'll forgive me for knocking you around." I

13
00:01:08.834 --> 00:01:14.230
didn't correct his memory as to who had knocked around whom, and we shook hands warmly. "You hadn't

14
00:01:14.231 --> 00:01:19.057
come into your good fortune when we met at Miss Havisham's?" asked Herbert. "No." "I myself was

15
00:01:19.058 --> 00:01:20.382
on the lookout for good fortune at that time. Miss Havisham had asked me to come for

16
00:01:20.383 --> 00:01:27.287
a trial visit. I suppose if she had liked me, I would have been provided for. Perhaps

17
00:01:27.288 --> 00:01:35.216
I might have even become engaged to Estella." "Weren't you disappointed?" I asked. "No. I didn't care much

18
00:01:35.217 --> 00:01:38.136
for her." "Miss Havisham?" "Well, I didn't care much for her either, but I meant Estella. That

19
00:01:38.137 --> 00:01:40.970
girl's cold and haughty, and Miss Havisham has brought her up to wreak revenge on all men."

20
00:01:40.971 --> 00:01:42.549
"How is Estella related to Miss Havisham? Why should she wreak revenge on all men? What revenge?" "Dear

21
00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:52.025
me, Pip! You have a lot of questions. Miss Havisham adopted Estella. The rest of the story

22
00:01:52.026 --> 00:01:59.350
will have to wait until dinnertime." Herbert showed me around his rooms—a sitting room plus two bedrooms.

23
00:01:59.351 --> 00:02:09.112
In the meantime he asked me about myself, and I told him the story of my good

24
00:02:09.113 --> 00:02:13.676
fortune, stressing that I was never to ask the name of my benefactor. Herbert knew that Mr. Jaggers

25
00:02:13.677 --> 00:02:22.359
was my guardian and that Jaggers was the one who'd recommended Matthew Pocket for my tutor. He

26
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:28.747
told me that Mr. Jaggers was Miss Havisham's lawyer and adviser. I liked Herbert at once. He

27
00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:33.065
was still a pale young gentleman, but there was something about him that was very charming. He seemed

28
00:02:33.066 --> 00:02:40.335
incapable of ever doing anything secretive or mean. There was something wonderfully hopeful about him, and yet

29
00:02:40.336 --> 00:02:44.676
at the same time I knew he would never be very successful or rich. When we sat

30
00:02:44.677 --> 00:02:52.002
down to dinner, I reminded Herbert of his promise to tell me about Miss Havisham. "She was

31
00:02:52.003 --> 00:02:57.160
a spoiled child. Her mother died when she was a baby, and her father denied her nothing. He

32
00:02:57.161 --> 00:03:03.390
was a country gentleman down in your part of the world, and he was a brewer. He

33
00:03:03.391 --> 00:03:07.811
was very rich and haughty, and his daughter was too." I guessed that she must have been

34
00:03:07.812 --> 00:03:11.874
an only child. But Herbert said that she had a younger half-brother. As the boy grew into a

35
00:03:11.875 --> 00:03:17.723
young man, he turned out to be so disrespectful and so wasteful that his father disinherited him.

36
00:03:17.724 --> 00:03:25.040
But when the father lay dying, he reconciled with his son and changed his will. So after

37
00:03:25.041 --> 00:03:29.882
the father died, the son was wealthy, though not as wealthy as Miss Havisham. "Miss Havisham was

38
00:03:29.883 --> 00:03:35.207
now an heiress," said Herbert. "Her half-brother had plenty of money again but wasted it with debts and

39
00:03:35.208 --> 00:03:43.612
bad judgment. He and Miss Havisham did not get along, and many people suspected that he held

40
00:03:43.613 --> 00:03:52.612
a grudge against her, blaming her for the time when his father was angry with him." Herbert

41
00:03:52.613 --> 00:03:59.167
continued his story. "There appeared on the scene a certain man who began to court Miss Havisham.

42
00:03:59.168 --> 00:04:06.183
He pursued her and claimed to be devoted to her. She loved him passionately and idolized him. He

43
00:04:06.184 --> 00:04:12.834
got huge sums of money from her and persuaded her to buy out her brother's share of

44
00:04:12.835 --> 00:04:18.167
the brewery at a great price. The idea was that when he was her husband, he would

45
00:04:18.168 --> 00:04:21.730
take care of it all and manage it for her." I could see that this story was going

46
00:04:21.731 --> 00:04:25.325
to end unhappily. "Why didn't Mr. Jaggers warn her against this man?" "He was not yet her

47
00:04:25.326 --> 00:04:34.055
lawyer. My father is her cousin, and he was the only person who warned her that she

48
00:04:34.056 --> 00:04:39.643
was doing too much for this man. Miss Havisham angrily ordered my father out of her house,

49
00:04:39.644 --> 00:04:42.309
and he has never seen her since." "So what happened?" I asked. "The wedding day was set; the

50
00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:49.634
wedding dress was bought; the wedding trip was planned out; the wedding guests were invited. The day

51
00:04:49.635 --> 00:04:54.888
came, but not the bridegroom. He wrote a letter—" "Which she received while she was dressing for

52
00:04:54.889 --> 00:05:01.293
her wedding!" I guessed. "At twenty minutes to nine!" "At that exact hour and minute," said Herbert, nodding.

53
00:05:01.294 --> 00:05:05.111
"Afterward she stopped all the clocks at that time. I do not know what was in the

54
00:05:05.112 --> 00:05:13.198
letter other than the fact that he broke off the marriage. Miss Havisham then took ill. When

55
00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:22.865
she recovered, she let the whole place fall into ruin, as you have seen, and she has

56
00:05:22.866 --> 00:05:27.770
never since looked upon the light of day." I sat, thinking about what he had told me. "Is

57
00:05:27.771 --> 00:05:32.111
that the whole story?" "All that I know of it. But I have forgotten one thing. It

58
00:05:32.112 --> 00:05:40.183
has long been suspected that her fiancé was acting with her half-brother, that it was a conspiracy

59
00:05:40.184 --> 00:05:42.349
between them and that they shared the profits." "What became of the two men?" "They fell into

60
00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:46.913
deeper shame—if there can be deeper—and ruin." "Are they alive now?" I asked. "I don't know, Pip." I

61
00:05:46.914 --> 00:05:50.159
had one more question for him. "You said that Estella was not related to Miss Havisham, but

62
00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.929
adopted. When was she adopted?" He shrugged. "It seems to me that Estella has always been there.

63
00:05:51.930 --> 00:06:00.588
And you now know everything that I know about Miss Havisham." "And you know everything that I know,"

64
00:06:00.589 --> 00:06:08.913
I retorted. "I believe that," said Herbert. "As to the condition on which you hold your advancement

65
00:06:08.914 --> 00:06:12.064
in life—namely that you are not to discuss or inquire about your benefactor—you may be sure that

66
00:06:12.065 --> 00:06:24.056
I will never mention it." He said this with so much sincerity that I believed we would

67
00:06:24.057 --> 00:06:31.620
never speak of it again, no matter how many years I lived under his father's roof. From the

68
00:06:31.621 --> 00:06:38.460
way he spoke, I also believed that we understood the same thing: Miss Havisham was my benefactor.

